bound-lock/1 about= at=policy:head/doors/0.1.7-guard by=target form=alphabet measure=id role=writes scope=device/head/coverage value=read/**|kept/**|act/**|access/**|use/**|refused/**
bound-lock/1 about="# A typed answer from a local API\n\nThis checkout example needs macOS and Node 22.18 or later. From the repository root, install its pinned dependencies with `npm ci --ignore-scripts`.\n\nThe existing test client speaks MCP, follows cards and handles the catalogue actions through the three public tools. This demo creates a temporary home, simulated keychain and loopback API. Its automatic yes applies only to this fixture. In normal use, you approve the connection yourself. The first run may fetch pinned public world artifacts; the API itself is local.\n\nRun this from the repository root:\n\n```sh\nnode --input-type=module <<'JS'\nimport { readFileSync, mkdtempSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { strict as assert } from 'node:assert';\nimport { api } from './test/two-sources.ts';\nimport { ask } from './test/client.ts';\nimport { fakeKeychain } from './test/fake-keychain.ts';\nimport { parse } from '@lapxo/topos/wire';\nconst demo = JSON.parse(readFileSync('docs/examples/first-minute.json', 'utf8'));\nconst fixture = await api(() => JSON.stringify(demo.response));\nconst home = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'head-example-'));\nconst env = { ...fakeKeychain({}).env, HEAD_HOME: join(home, 'home'), HOME: home, USERPROFILE: home };\nconst call = (name, arguments_) => ({ method: 'tools/call', params: { name, arguments: arguments_ } });\ntry {\n  const results = await ask([\n    call('connect', { source: demo.source, document: fixture.document }),\n    call('ask', { place: join(home, 'place'), source: demo.source, operation: demo.operation, args: demo.args })\n  ], [], env, () => ({ action: 'accept', content: { yes: true } }));\n  const output = results.at(-1).result.content[0].text;\n  const lines = output.split('\\n').map(parse).filter(p => p.kind === 'fact').map(p => p.value.fields);\n  for (const [field, value] of Object.entries(demo.expectedTypedValues))\n    assert(lines.some(line => line.scope?.endsWith('/' + field) && line.value === value));\n  assert(lines.some(line => line.scope?.startsWith('read/forecast/') && line.measure === 'digest' && line.sig));\n  console.log(JSON.stringify({ source: demo.source, values: Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(demo.expectedTypedValues).map(field => [field, lines.find(line => line.scope?.endsWith('/' + field)).value])), receipt: lines.find(line => line.scope?.startsWith('read/forecast/') && line.measure === 'digest').value }, null, 2));\n} finally { fixture.close(); }\nJS\n```\n\nThe returned fields include `temperature = 21` and `sky = clear`, plus the read's signed digest receipt. Paths and signatures vary; those are observations, not fixed demo bytes. The fixture schema supplies the types. Another API with no usable schema is named untyped instead of receiving invented types.\n\nTo ask a real source, supply its document in your assistant, choose a source name, and approve its connection. An overlay can fill in what the provider declares separately. Access is anonymous only when permitted, or obtained by the declared flow or secret-input path. Never enter a secret in the model conversation. See [access options](connect-any-api.md).\n\nThe separate timing acceptance is `HEAD_ONBOARDING=1 HEAD_FIRST_CELL_STRICT=1 node --test test/onboarding.test.ts`. Running this demo is not a timing or platform certification.\n" at=policy:head/documentation/0.2.0 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/page/first-minute shape=docs/first-minute.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Agreement keeps its sources\n\nTwo matching answers need not be two independent observations. head compares typed fields, keeps each source and its read receipt, and displays declared lineage rather than inventing a confidence score.\n\n| Marks | What the view says |\n|---|---|\n| `●○` | A later source shares declared lineage or a recognized source identity with an earlier one. |\n| `●●` | Separate source marks in this view. Unknown or undeclared lineage is not proof of independence. |\n\nIf values differ, head shows each source's value and time. It does not pick a winner or manufacture a numeric loss. A field missing from one source is partial evidence, not disagreement with an assertion nobody made.\n\nThis checkout example uses the existing two-source fixture and declaration/withdrawal mechanism. The fixture sources return the same two fields; these are not measurements or an independence experiment. Run `npm ci --ignore-scripts` first, then:\n\n```sh\nnode --input-type=module <<'JS'\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { strict as assert } from 'node:assert';\nimport { api, person } from './test/two-sources.ts';\nconst demo = JSON.parse(readFileSync('docs/examples/lineage.json', 'utf8'));\nconst a = await api(() => JSON.stringify(demo.response));\nconst b = await api(() => JSON.stringify(demo.response));\ntry {\n  const p = await person(a.document, b.document);\n  p.rooted([demo.declare]);\n  const grouped = await p.asked();\n  assert(grouped.length > 0 && grouped.every(c => c.code === demo.before.code && c.fields.marks === demo.before.marks));\n  const receipts = new Set(grouped.flatMap(c => c.fields.receipts.split('|')));\n  assert(grouped.every(c => c.fields.sources === 'forecast, mirror' && c.fields.receipts.split('|').length === 2));\n  assert([...receipts].every(receipt => p.receipts().has(receipt)));\n  p.rooted([demo.withdraw]);\n  const after = await p.asked();\n  assert(after.length > 0 && after.every(c => c.code === demo.after.code && c.fields.marks === demo.after.marks));\n  assert(after.every(c => c.fields.sources === 'forecast, mirror'));\n  assert([...receipts].every(receipt => p.receipts().has(receipt)));\n  console.log(JSON.stringify({ before: demo.before, after: demo.after, oldReceiptsRetained: true, independenceProved: false }, null, 2));\n} finally { a.close(); b.close(); }\nJS\n```\n\nThe declaration says that `mirror` shares `forecast`'s lineage. Its withdrawal removes that current claim, not the sources or old receipts. The later `●●` is reported as **unknown lineage**, not newfound independence. Each mark still points to a source-specific read; grouping does not merge receipt identities.\n" at=policy:head/documentation/0.2.0 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/page/lineage shape=docs/lineage.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Compatibility\n\nThe desktop target for 0.2.3 remains macOS 13.5 or later. Node is not included in the package. A normal Node client runs Head directly. Under Electron, Head relaunches once with an installed Node >=22.18, inherited stdio, a sanitized environment and forwarded termination signals. If none is available, `runtime/node-required` refuses before key lookup or founding. There is no download or automatic installation.\n\nThe command-line package and the desktop extension use the same algebra, signing protocol, permission checks and source ports. A client name does not select a security policy. A runtime version alone does not establish that the client permits child processes.\n\n## Open platform work\n\n| Issue | Platform | Required before support |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P1 | Linux | A signing-store adapter that distinguishes missing from failed lookup and guarantees create-if-absent; real Secret Service, browser and installed-runtime acceptance |\n| P2 | Windows | A signing-store adapter with those same guarantees; real Credential Manager, browser and installed-runtime acceptance |\n\nThe existing adapters can still be checked as command interfaces. Their presence does not advertise support, and credential update is never a substitute for exclusive signing-key creation. An unsupported environment refuses before key lookup or founding a place.\n\nReal-client, system-keychain and independent Intel execution acceptance remain pending. Neither a simulated Electron process nor CI establishes those integrations.\n" at=policy:head/system-node-successors/0.2.3 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/page/compatibility shape=docs/compatibility.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Connect any API\n\nhead reads an API by the document its provider already publishes, at the address you give it. head names no provider and connects none of its own: what is connected is what you hand it. Here is one way for each level of access, with an API of each kind.\n\n### No access: a document that says nothing about it\n\nTake an API whose document lists its operations and states nothing about access. head does not guess what that means: it tries a read once with nothing attached, and keeps what it saw (`access/<source>/<operation>/observed anonymous`) as a receipt its own key signs. Nothing but a read is ever tried this way.\n\nConnect the document by its address, `https://<provider>/openapi.yaml`, by the site that publishes it, or by a folder holding it, as `forecast`, then read one of its operations. With no place yet, one yes founds your place in `~/place`, connects the document there and lets head read `forecast` for the next 90 days. A document that names several servers asks once which one. The answer comes back typed by what the document declares, one value a line, with a signed receipt of the read (abridged):\n\n```text\nanswer/<request>/current/temperature   number  …\nanswer/<request>/current_units/temperature   text    …\nread/forecast/<operation>/<request>    digest  sha256:… signed by head\n```\n\nAn overlay can still say what the provider says on its own site, so the place states it rather than observes it:\n\n```yaml\noverlay: 1.0.0\ninfo: { title: the forecast, read without a key, version: '1' }\nactions:\n  - target: \"$.paths['/forecast'].get\"\n    description: the provider says this read needs no key\n    update: { security: [] }\n```\n\nWhatever API you connect, its terms are the provider's: read them before you connect it, and say where an answer came from when they ask you to.\n\n### A declared flow: OAuth 2.0\n\nTake an API whose document declares OAuth 2.0 with an authorization code. head compiles it into the two steps it takes, the provider's page with PKCE and then the token exchange, and nobody writes a line of it. What it needs is yours: the client id of an app you registered with the provider, signed into the place as `keys/access/<source>/<flow> client <id>`. Until then head says so and calls nothing.\n\n### A secret entered by hand\n\nWhen a document declares a bearer token or an API key and no way to obtain one, the key is a secret input. head asks for it on its own one-time page, never through the model, and only when the place's root allows a secret with `access/secret-inputs`. It is kept in the keychain, never in a file.\n\n### When the document says too little\n\nA login of its own (an email, a one-time code, a choice of account) is written once as an `x-access` flow, an Arazzo profile in an overlay, and every API of the same issuer refers to it with one `$ref`. The profile and its vectors are on topos-openapi's own page, `docs/x-access.md` in `@lapxo/topos-openapi`. Every API connected this way is read the same way afterwards: one access per issuer, each answer typed and kept.\n" at=policy:head/connect/0.1.4 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/page/connect-any-api shape=docs/connect-any-api.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Security\n\nReport vulnerabilities privately to hello@lapxo.com. Do not open public issues for security reports.\n\nReports are reviewed on a best-effort basis. We do not guarantee a response or remediation deadline. There is no bug bounty or reward. With your consent, we may credit a report in a published fix.\n\n## What is trusted\n\nThe owner chooses the place, its admitted keys and permissions, the installed instrument, the source descriptions and the transport policy. The operating system, keychain and code running with the user's privileges are part of the trusted computing base. The model, remote source content, received packages and suggested actions are not authority. The MCP client is trusted to display consent requests and report the person's response accurately; a compromised or auto-approving client can misrepresent that response, and head cannot prove a person clicked yes.\n\nA source document describes an operation. Its accuracy and the source's data remain the provider's responsibility. A misclassified read can have server-side effects; head does not prove the provider's implementation. A signature proves the statement checked under its admitted key and context; it does not prove that an API answer is true.\n\n## Signing and access secrets\n\nSigning keys are handled by the short-lived signer. Its supported interface returns public keys or signatures, and the long process refuses a signing key requested by name. API credentials are different: the access and transport adapters must handle them to authenticate the request to the declared provider. Supported paths keep them out of model arguments, normal answers, command arguments and ordinary receipts.\n\nThis is a code boundary, not OS isolation. The signer and head run as the same user. A compromised same-user process can invoke the keychain, replace code or read data that user can access. This release does not claim protection from it, nor guaranteed secret removal from arbitrary malicious source bodies, crash dumps or external programs. Do not put secrets in API descriptions or model messages.\n\n## Network and doors\n\nA digest is not network permission. Retrieval still requires the receiver's origin and transport policy. The declared host checks destinations and redirect policy; a matching hash does not authorize a private-network request, a downgrade or forwarding credentials to another origin.\n\nThe network doors bind to loopback and validate their Host and Origin boundaries. Loopback alone is not authentication. A client can suggest an action, but the place's permissions and consent rules still apply. Cancellation and request scratch cleanup have their existing bounds; they do not make an arbitrary process safe to run.\n\n## Configuration is not general execution authority\n\nThe native authority preflight checks exact configuration records and their admission context. It authenticates the selected place, instrument, key, records, epoch and explicit clock. A card is an observation of that context, not permission to sign or land a changed future action. The actual act independently checks current authority.\n\nAPI execution, aggregates, access negotiation, secret consent and personal-attestation admission are not all covered by that preflight. Their existing domain checks remain. An old card can become stale after any Root epoch change even when a particular key's eligibility remains unchanged. A local coverage declaration is not revocation of the original Root anchor.\n\n## Sharing and lineage\n\nA content reference, source package and portable public birth declaration are different objects. Inspection does not connect a source, import grants or install an owner. A holder address is not authenticated sender identity. Hash equality establishes the named bytes only under the selected contract.\n\nSource descriptions can themselves contain sensitive information. Review them before sharing. The selected portable birth profile excludes authority material; it does not make every file in a place public. Keep the local birth journal, credentials, keychain, admission history and derived stores out of a shared package.\n\nLineage marks report declared relationships and recognized source identity. They do not establish statistical independence, a confidence score or a count of independent causes. Withdrawing a declaration changes the current view; it does not erase earlier receipts.\n\n## Supported environment and useful reports\n\nmacOS desktop is the supported environment. Linux and Windows remain open as P1 and P2 in the [compatibility record](docs/compatibility.md). Other platform adapters and tests are not evidence of a real secret service, browser flow or unattended deployment. Multiple local processes do not establish distributed coordination over a shared object store.\n\nUseful reports include a reproducible action that obtains a key without coverage, lands without an admitted signer, bypasses a door or network policy, changes a refusal through a supported door, or treats received bytes or a stale card as permission. Include the version, expected boundary and a minimal reproducer. Redact credentials and personal paths; never send a signing key.\n\nThe model's behavior, third-party API correctness and programs outside the supported boundaries are not security guarantees of head. A vulnerability in how head handles those inputs is still relevant. Related algebra, contract and instrument bugs can be reported through the same private channel.\n" at=policy:head/standalone-runtime/0.2.3 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/security shape=SECURITY.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Share public declarations, then inspect them\n\nA place has several different things you can share:\n\n- A source package names its documents, overlays and compiled pins by digest. Receiving it still needs local permission.\n- A content reference names bytes. A separate holder location says where to obtain them; no location means no invented address. A viewer address is not a holder.\n- `birth.topos` is a separate unsigned public declaration artifact. It does not carry the local `birth.bound`, keys, grants or admission history.\n\nThis executable checkout example shares only a public language declaration. It creates independent fixture places and simulated keys. It does not contact an external API or change your real keychain. Run `npm ci --ignore-scripts` first; Node 22.18 or later is required.\n\n```sh\nnode --input-type=module <<'JS'\nimport { readFileSync, mkdtempSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { strict as assert } from 'node:assert';\nimport { born, line } from './test/place.ts';\nimport { ask } from './test/client.ts';\nconst demo = JSON.parse(readFileSync('docs/examples/sharing.json', 'utf8'));\nconst shared = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'head-public-example-'));\nconst sender = born(undefined, [line(demo.sender.scope, 'id', demo.sender.value)], null, false, 'catalog', false);\nconst receiver = born(undefined, [line(demo.receiver.scope, 'id', demo.receiver.value)], null, false, 'route-notes', false);\nconst env = p => ({ ...p.keychain.env, HEAD_HOME: join(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'head-example-home-')), 'home') });\nconst call = (name, arguments_) => ({ method: 'tools/call', params: { name, arguments: arguments_ } });\nconst [packed] = await ask([call('share', { place: sender.place, to: join(shared, 'public') })], [shared], env(sender));\nconst birth = packed.result.structuredContent.cells.find(c => c.code === 'head/birth-shared');\nassert(birth);\nconst before = readFileSync(join(receiver.place, 'TARGET.bound'));\nconst [inspected, altered] = await ask([\n  call('connect', { place: receiver.place, document: birth.fields.document, digest: birth.fields.digest }),\n  call('connect', { place: receiver.place, document: birth.fields.record + ' ', digest: birth.fields.digest })\n], [shared], env(receiver));\nconst observation = inspected.result.structuredContent.cells.find(c => c.code === demo.expectedCode);\nassert(observation && observation.fields.collisions === demo.expectedCollision);\nassert(altered.result.structuredContent.cells.some(c => c.code === demo.negativeCode));\nassert.deepEqual(readFileSync(join(receiver.place, 'TARGET.bound')), before);\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify({ inspection: observation.code, collision: observation.fields.collisions, alteredBytes: demo.negativeCode, authorityTransferred: false }, null, 2));\nJS\n```\n\nThe receiver names the occupied `lang` coordinate and keeps its own value. One changed byte with the expected digest is refused. Inspection does not import permission or identify an authenticated sender. To adopt a declaration, the receiver uses its ordinary local admission process; this example intentionally does not do that.\n\nA standing reference follows the same separation: compare the expected content identity and apply the receiver's network policy before fetching. A holder URL is not authority. The existing located-reference vector is `node --test test/link-url.test.ts`; it also covers missing locations, altered bytes and denied transport. Review source documents for sensitive information before sharing them.\n" at=policy:head/documentation/0.2.0 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/page/sharing shape=docs/sharing.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# head\n\n[![npm](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/@lapxo/head)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@lapxo/head) [![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue)](LICENSE)\n\nTurn API documents and MCP connections into your own MCP extension. Keep who answered, what was read, and what you allowed.\n\nHead starts empty. You choose the sources; the recipient uses their own credentials. Sharing public descriptions does not transfer your permissions, signing keys, tokens or API responses.\n\n```text\nAPI documents + MCP endpoints → Head → your custom .mcpb\n                                      → recipient approves and signs in\n```\n\n1. Connect the sources you want and approve each connection.\n2. Ask questions or use permitted operations, with receipts.\n3. Ask Head to package the connected place using `do` with `action: \"share\"`. Give the recipient the resulting `.mcpb`.\n\n[Export a connected place](#export-a-connected-place) · [Try a local example](docs/first-minute.md) · [Understand the boundaries](SECURITY.md)\n\nhead is an MCP server. Give it an API document, approve the connection, and ask a question. It reads operations the document declares, returns typed fields when a schema is available, and keeps signed receipts of its reads. Without a usable schema, the answer is named as untyped.\n\n```text\nYou: Connect this API as forecast.\n     [give its OpenAPI document; approve the connection]\nYou: What is the forecast in Lima?\n\nforecast: temperature 21, sky clear\nreceipt: sha256:…\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative fixture, not a live forecast. [Run the local example](docs/first-minute.md) without an account or a real secret.\n\n## Start\n\nFor an assistant that starts MCP servers by command:\n\n```sh\nnpx -y @lapxo/head@0.2.3 run\n```\n\nThe pin above names this release; the executable examples in this tree use the checkout. A client can also open a desktop extension from [releases](https://github.com/Lapxo/head/releases); check the artifact digests supplied with the release. Starting head alone prints help; `head run` starts the MCP server.\n\nThe desktop target is macOS 13.5 or later. Head does not bundle Node. The extension uses the client runtime; when that runtime is Electron, Head relaunches once using an installed Node 22.18 or later so its signer and child processes work. Without a compatible installed Node, its three tools explain what is missing with `head/runtime-node-required`; no place, keychain or connection is opened. The command-line installation also requires Node 22.18 or later. Linux and Windows remain open as P1 and P2 in the [compatibility record](docs/compatibility.md), alongside independent real-client and Intel execution acceptance. Unattended cloud execution is unsupported. A successful mock or CI job does not establish working system-keychain and browser integration.\n\n## What you do\n\n- **Connect** a document, optionally with an overlay. head pins the compiled description by digest. With no place yet, the first approved connection creates `~/place`.\n- **Ask** for one operation or compare the same question across sources. Receipts keep the source and time. A missing answer stays owed; an empty answer is not a missing answer.\n- **Act** within the place's permissions and consent rules. A suggested next step is not authorization. Writes do not become allowed because a model asked for them.\n- **Share and inspect** public material. A reference identifies content; a separate holder location says where to obtain it. Inspection checks the selected contract and grants nothing. [Try sharing and verification](docs/sharing.md).\n\nYour place keeps its declarations as text. `birth.bound` separates your local declarations from head's profile. Keep it private unless you have reviewed what you are sharing: it is not the portable public `birth.topos` artifact.\n\n## Two names are not two independent sources\n\nIf `mirror` shares `forecast`'s declared lineage, agreement is shown as `●○`. Both sources and their receipts remain visible. `●●` does not prove independence; it only reports what the declared lineage and source identity establish. Disagreement keeps each source's value instead of choosing a winner.\n\n[Run the lineage example](docs/lineage.md), including withdrawal of the lineage declaration without erasing earlier receipts.\n\n## Boundaries you can check\n\nSigning keys stay behind a short-lived signer port in the supported flow. Tokens go to the declared transport, not into model arguments. A compromised process running as your user is outside that code boundary: it can invoke the keychain itself.\n\nA digest is not network permission or proof that an answer is true. Received bytes install no authority. Configuration preflight does not replace API permission, access or secret-consent checks. See the [threat model and reporting policy](SECURITY.md), and [connect an API](docs/connect-any-api.md) for access options.\n\nThe three public MCP tools are `ask`, `connect` and `do`. Examples reuse the existing client and fixtures; they add no provider-specific behavior to head.\n\n### Export a connected place\n\nHead's base extension contains no connected sources. Sharing a place never silently creates an empty extension. Provide explicit public package identity, revision, title, description and author; Head selects all currently connected sources and their declared operations when `sources` is omitted:\n\n```json\n{\"action\":\"share\",\"place\":\"/your/place\",\"to\":\"/your/shared/team.mcpb\",\"args\":{\"id\":\"team\",\"revision\":\"1.0.0\",\"title\":\"Team sources\",\"description\":\"Selected public documents\",\"author\":{\"name\":\"Your public name\"}}}\n```\n\nAn explicit `sources` selection narrows that snapshot. Source and operation budgets still apply; nothing is silently truncated. Missing public package metadata produces `bundle/metadata-required`. The metadata grants no authority. The recipient accepts the named package, chooses an origin where needed, and supplies their own credentials. Export only documents and overlays you intend to make public.\n\nRepeat exports preserve member paths and content under identical inputs. The standard MCPB packager includes ZIP timestamps; identical container bytes across different times are not promised. Each release approval identifies one exact archive.\n\nAccess credentials are bound to the authenticated place, origin and declared access context. Receipt rejection returns no usable attachment. An interrupted or unrecorded attempt is not automatically retried: inspect it first, then explicitly request `access` with `args: {\"retry\":true}` if appropriate. Existing signing keys are never migrated. A compatible legacy home namespace requires explicit local approval after its public keys match the authenticated home.\n" at=policy:head/messageport-successors/0.2.3 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/readme shape=README.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="*.mts\n*changelog*\n!node_modules/@lapxo/bound/**/*.d.ts\n" at=policy:head/bundle-pin/0.1.7 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/bundle/exclusions shape=.mcpbignore value=lock
